rgence  from haftar's forces  i think it it's important not to get  carried away with i know there's a lot  of people very happy with  uh the end of hafta's military solution  and anybody who wants to negotiate a  political solution  should celebrate the fact that after uh  tripoli offensive uh has failed  however as you said it's important to  put it into context this is the end of  hefter's bid to completely win  in the civil war this is what we're  seeing today is a return to the status  quo  the stalemate that we saw for many years  between east and between west  and this prospect of a military campaign  eastwards is very unlikely and the  reason i say this is that  the the biggest dynamic is not  necessarily in the military terms  the biggest dynamic is the changing in  power brokers it's no longer eu it's no  longer european countries  it's no longer uae or egypt or these  countries it's now russia  on the eastern side and turkey on the  western side almost a repeat of syria in  the sense that  these are the countries that are going  to be pushing the parties to decide what  the future of libya  should look like and that's why we're  hearing talks of partition talks of  autonomous regions  talks of a status quo whereby west will  be turkey influence gna  and east will be tubrik influenced  russia  so in other words what we're seeing  today is not a complete  alt alternating of the dynamics of the  war we're seeing a return to the status  quo  that was in 2019 before the start of the  tripoli offensive  and the road ahead to find some sort of  solution to the war  is still very long and if anything even  more complicated now  because of the ferocity of the  international struggle  a resurgent us a very angry france a  confused germany  and italy that is scrambling russia  which is asserting itself  turkey which is now the dominant power  in other words it looks far more  complicated and far more complex than it  looked  three two or three years ago let's talk  